✗ cross-module import

    internal/modules/notes/application/attach.go:3
      package example.com/fix/internal/modules/notes/application
        imports example.com/fix/internal/modules/tasks/domain

  This reaches into another feature module's internals. Modules talk
  through published events or an exported port, never by importing each
  other's packages — that is what makes extracting one into its own
  service a wiring change rather than a rewrite.

  Fix one of:
    • If this feature is REACTING to something that happened in the
      other, consume its EVENT. `warren g consumer` writes the handler
      and the subscription. The event is a wire contract, so the other
      feature stays extractable. This is the preferred answer.
    • If this feature needs to ASK the other something, the port has to
      live where both may see it — which is NOT inside either feature.
      Move the interface to a self-contained package outside
      internal/modules/:

          internal/contracts/<owner>/  — the interface and its own
          request and result types, importing no feature package.

      The owner's infrastructure implements it, the owner's module
      exports it with warren.Exports[...](), and this feature imports
      the contract package and the owner's module value in module.go.
      A contract package that imports a feature is reported here too,
      as a cross-module import through a helper.

1 violation(s) in 4 packages.

  Checked 2 feature modules for cross-module imports.
